1: # Plan 6: prepared unbounded support and proven scalar preludes
2: 
3: Status: compiler prerequisite passed all 17 native suites. Nonanchored executor
4: admission and numerical corrections are implemented; expanded acceptance tests
5: and package verification are in progress after source review. The command
6: automasking gate is complete. This slice handles nonanchored unbounded commands;
7: anchored grab, preview, dyntopo, general attributes and other host hooks remain
8: explicit subsequent dependencies of the full Plan 6 gate.
9: 
10: ## Current implementation constraints
11: 
12: - Kelvinlet is both unbounded and grab-capable. Anchored mode chooses
13:   AccumOrigGrab and requires first-touch image arbitration; this slice keeps the
14:   anchored guard. Nonanchored Kelvinlet still has a generated host clamp and a
15:   scalar reduce prelude, so merely removing the unbounded guard is insufficient.
16: - The compiler marks every host/reduce stage unsafe. Its assignment checker only
17:   admits lexical value locals and direct vertex geometry writes. Kelvinlet's
18:   host writes mu/nu, whose exact declared domains already guarantee those clamps
19:   are no-ops after successful prepared scalar validation. Its reduce computes
20:   two scalar outputs from those values without touching shared state.
21: - An unbounded field has an optional cutoff at radius * unboundedExtent. Extent
22:   <= 0 disables the cutoff. A conservative all-leaf query is correct for both;
23:   a radius-only query truncates later unbounded stages. Cavity first contact must
24:   cover the field's actual support, rather than the regular brush sphere.
25: 
26: ## Implementation
27: 
28: 1. Add conservative compiler proofs for two narrowly defined preludes.
29:    A host body qualifies only when it contains canonical scalar-uniform range
30:    clamps: `if (field > declared_max) field = declared_max` or the corresponding
31:    lower-bound form, with no else, extra writes, unknown calls, loops or context
32:    access. Restrict the initial proof to FLOAT32 fields with explicit finite
33:    ranges, matching comparison/assignment constants exactly. Normalize domains
34:    with the same representable-bound rules used at preparation. All other host
35:    stages remain ineligible. Preserve original host code for raw execution and
36:    retain its call during prepared execution, where the proof guarantees no change.
37: 2. A reduce prelude qualifies only for straight-line scalar local/output
38:    assignments from literals, current scalar uniforms, already initialized locals
39:    or earlier initialized outputs, using pure arithmetic expressions. Require
40:    every output initialized before use and before completion. Reject shared writes,
41:    input-parameter writes, unknown calls, loops, neighbor/geometry/context reads
42:    and ambiguous shadowing. Keep general compiler behavior unchanged; this proof
43:    controls prepared eligibility only. Combine these proofs with the existing
44:    assignment/call audit; never allow a safe prelude to hide unsafe vertex writes.
45:    Emit default-false command metadata for proven no-op host stages and include
46:    proven reduce stages in preparedScalarSafe. Do not whitelist a tool name.
47: 3. Snapshot unboundedExtent independently for every prepared stage, including
48:    stages whose kernel does not declare it, so an earlier declaring command cannot
49:    leak its working value. If the current manifest declares it, use its prepared
50:    typed value. Otherwise inherit the authoritative Brush member without adding
51:    a persistent property or allowing an undeclared scalar override. Apply the
52:    stage snapshot and restore the original working member with existing scopes.
53: 4. After full scalar/metadata validation, validate each positive-radius unbounded
54:    stage's finite extent and finite positive cutoff arithmetic when enabled.
55:    Keep zero-radius stages skipped. A program with any executing unbounded stage
56:    selects all nonempty mesh/grid leaves; bounded-only programs retain the existing
57:    maximum-radius query. Do not mutate query lists, publish, capture, change
58:    topology or allocate persistent attributes on a rejected candidate.
59: 5. Admit proven safe nonanchored unbounded commands in both prepared executors.
60:    Execute the already-prepared command and prelude without legacy loadProps or
61:    a second dynamics evaluation. Cavity contact for an unbounded stage is strictly
62:    inside its positive cutoff, or every visited vertex when cutoff is disabled.
63:    Bounded stage contact remains its own spherical support even in an all-leaf
64:    shared program. Keep the stage column valid outside contact as before.
65: 
66: ## Acceptance
67: 
68: - Compiler positive/negative fixtures cover each permitted syntax, nearby unsafe
69:   variants, shadowing, assignments hidden in index expressions/calls, output
70:   initialization, mixed safe/unsafe stages and built-in Kelvinlet generation.
71:   Existing compiler/backend checks must continue to pass; regenerate native/TS
72:   declarations and generated kernels through the dispatcher.
73: - Mesh live/CSR and grid immediate/deferred tests compare nonanchored standalone
74:   and program Kelvinlet with independent raw execution over all leaves. Include
75:   DRAW followed by a larger unbounded field, changed pressure and material stacks,
76:   zero/missed first dabs, cutoff disabled, exact boundary, growing support, and
77:   subsequent bounded strokes. Use multiple leaves and vertices outside the main
78:   brush's original region. Demonstrate actual movement there.
79: - Test invalid extent/product/late-stage candidates before topology, undo,
80:   counters, properties and query-state mutation. Command extent inheritance and
81:   scoped restoration must survive success, miss, zero radius and rejection.
82: - Cavity-on unbounded stages must match independent cutoff-support oracles and
83:   keep first-contact values for subsequent dabs. Different scalar material values
84:   must not create accidental cavity configuration changes.
85: - Exercise required-prepared C API/Python calls and input batches with the current
86:   nonanchored geometric context. Test native undo/redo and installed package
87:   provenance. Do not claim anchored modal adoption from this bounded slice.
88: 
89: The all-leaf query is a deliberate correctness-first choice for unbounded stages.
90: It avoids support truncation without extending today's pinned-grab lifetime
91: rules. A later measured optimization may use a finite validated extent query.
92: 
93: ## Implementation review corrections — 2026-09-18
94: 
95: The execution source review requires reflected `grabFrom`, `grabTo`, and `viewDir`
96: for each prepared batch image. A scoped image frame restores the primary frame
97: on success and failure; signs are already restricted to exactly -1 or +1 by
98: batch input validation. Raw batch behavior retains its existing frame policy.
99: Compare mirrored batches against explicitly reflected single calls in the DLL.
100: 
101: Strengthen native coverage with both mesh neighbor modes, nonconstant cavity
102: factors independently computed at first contact, exact cutoff exclusion, changing
103: material settings without changing cache identity, and all finite-product/vector
104: rejection classes before geometry, topology, query, or cavity-cache mutation.
105: Add actual late-stage extent isolation and zero/miss/subsequent bounded execution
106: before claiming the complete unbounded gate. Snapshot-only tests do not satisfy
107: the executing extent-isolation requirement.
108: 
109: The reported `ctx` parameter collision is already rejected by the lexer because
110: `ctx` and `brush` are reserved tokens. The emitter also rejects those names
111: defensively for programmatically constructed IR; generated internal names remain
112: covered by compiler tests. This does not require expanding eligible prelude syntax.
113: 
114: ## Review dispositions — 2026-09-18
115: 
116: The [compiler review](../tests/plan6-unbounded-compiler-review.md) and
